A||||9||saleenfiero||Stereo guys only!!!||||7||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||03-11-2001||10:45 PM||||Okay, now that we're alone.  Do any of you people with aftermarket stereos, high watt amps and lots of juice needed, what kind of mods (if any) have you done elec system wise?<BR>EX. high output alternator, dual battery setup?<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||7
Z||000001||Philphine||03-11-2001||11:43 PM||||nothing beyond a good power lead to the stereo amps. but then i'm only using 150 watts.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MoneypitGT||03-12-2001||12:10 AM||||More than one battery won't do anything for you unless the car isn't running, or you have enough of a current draw to drop below 12 vots with the car running (3 or more BIG amps usually). Get a decent alternator, a cap or two and you should be fine. I have two amps, and a cap, voltage is fine, about 500wrms total. Check out my install below, not the best I have done, but my other GT is the project... I am building kicks and doing video, after the motor swap is done.<BR>          Chris<P><BR>------------------<BR><B>Check out my site at:</B> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fieroproject" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fieroproject</A> <BR>2 '87 GT Projects<p>[This message has been edited by MoneypitGT (edited 03-12-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000003||billyboy||03-12-2001||03:17 AM||||Well, in my old car, I had a 90 amp alternator and fried it 3 times before I got smart.  I had 2 200w amps, one bridged to 2 ohms pushing 2 subs, the other running 4 x 50 in to the front components and rear fill speakers.  When it's 90-100 degrees outside and raining, I had my AC on, windshield wipers, headlights, stereo, etc. and fried my alternator.  Happend that way twice, the other time it was just chance.  Finally I took the alternator to my local automotive electronics repair shop, and they rebuilt it to be a 130 amp alternator rather than a 90 amp.  Also, I bought one of those Stinger Gel-Cells to replace my battery.  After that, I didn't have any problems at all.<P>You may want to think about getting the alternator rebuilt, but capacitors will reduce the load on the battery and alternator as a general rule of thumb.  It all depends on how nuts you want to get with it.  Unfortunately the Fiero doesn't allow getting too crazy with the sound system without an extreme amount of custom fabrication.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||03-12-2001||10:46 AM||||I have installed a lot of high power systems. First, make sure you use the correct size power and gound wires. Make sure that all the amps are gounded at the same point. Don't have one amp grounded on one side of the car and the other amp to the other side. This could create a "ground loop" and induce noise. Also, try and keep the gound wire shorter on length than the power wire. Length adds resistance. The less resistance going to ground the less noise that can be induced. For subs with a lot of power you can hook up a stiffening capacitor. This will store power (current) and release it faster than the alternator or battery.<BR>As for alternators, You could buy one of the very expensive high-output ones or save your self a few hundred dollars and remove your original alternator and have it rewound for higher power. I had a car that was pushing close to 1800W and I had the alt rewound to 150A. The cost was only $90. Dual batteries is OK But if the alternator cannot supply the current to properly run the amps, it will reduce the charge rate of the batteries.<BR>Finally, once the system is in and functioning, Hook up a volt meter (or watch the gage if the car has one) Turn up the stereo then turn your lights on (engine running) and see if the lights flicker with the beat of the music. If it does you are taxing the electrical system and need to upgrade it with one or all of the above methods. Sometimes just adding the caps corrects the problem if you are at 1000W or less. Anything over 1000W I would get the alt rewound.<P>Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||saleenfiero||03-12-2001||11:44 AM||||Cool guys thanx.  I'm not running any super power stereo here, but it is still enough.  One 300W and a 220W so I'll evaluate what needs to be done.  <P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||03-12-2001||11:59 AM||||running the set up you have a 300w(30amp) and a 220(25amp) thats a 55 amp load on your alternator not including what the car already pulls from it. you can get a hold of an alternator/starter rebuilder in your area (not NAPA or AutoZone, Etc) an have them change the voltage regulator and re-clock the back of the alternator. a 130amp unit will run the car and the audio equipment. thats what i had to do. or get a hold of Street Wires or Scoche(sp) they make larger units that'll work. 141.3db at 43hz.<P>Xtant 1001Dx w/lfq-45 1000wrms x1 @ 1ohm <A HREF="http://www.xtant.com" TARGET=_blank>www.xtant.com</A> <BR>Kenwood Kac-626 50x2<BR>Sony CDX-C90<BR>Kicker Solobaric 10" at 2ohm<BR>a pair of MB Quart 4" in the dash <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg</A>   <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstxtant.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstxtant.jpg</A>  - my new one...<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 03-12-2001).]||||reg||1
